00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:46.539
competition, which they won, and the Heritage
Health prediction competition, which they

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:47.619
got 12th place in.

00:01:47.619 --> 00:01:51.049
So congratulations on those.

00:01:51.049 --> 00:01:56.390
Dr. Caffo has been given numerous awards,
and I list a couple of them here.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:01.960
The first one is the PECASE award, which is
the Presidential Early Career Award for Scientists

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:02.960
and Engineers.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:09.039
This is probably the most prestigious award
that can be given to an early career researcher.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:14.510
He's also won awards for teaching and for
mentoring.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:21.991
So with that, I will turn it over to Dr. Brian
Caffo.

00:02:21.991 --> 00:02:25.300
Brian, thank you very much for being with
us today.

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:27.400
Thanks, Michelle.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.830
I hope everyone can hear me.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.780
Thank you for inviting me.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:36.050
And thanks, Crystal, for doing all the organization.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:41.190
So my topic today is exploratory data analysis.

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:47.730
And I'm going to talk about this more like
an instructor of an exploratory data analysis,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:52.260
because it's an area that I use a lot, but
it's certainly not an area that I do a lot

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.040
of active research in.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:58.590
I do have one notable exception.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:04.310
My former PhD student, named Bruce Swihart,
who's a really brilliant guy, he works at

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:07.390
the NIH now.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:17.170
He wrote a paper that was really about contributing
the idea of using heat maps to perform visualization

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:18.480
for longitudinal data analysis.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:24.080
And he has probably the best title of any
paper I've ever written, for certain, called

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.540
Lasagna Plots, a Saucy Alternative to Spaghetti
Plots.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:31.100
So if you want to see a nice little paper
on visualization, check out Bruce's paper.

00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:35.950
But the fundamental idea-- I'll just cover
this really quickly-- is that with spaghetti

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.780
plots, like in this-- hopefully everyone can
see my cursor.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.750
If you can't, maybe someone will let me know.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.730
Like, this big, black mass over here is
just a lot of overplotting from spaghetti

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:47.730
plots.

00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:54.000
In cases like this, you might want to do something
like put it into a heat map where you could

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.070
actually see trends.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:04:00.870
Like, here, we were investigating sleep disorder
breathing and some EEG signals.

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.090
And you see some interesting missing data
patterns.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.680
You also see just generally that the sleep
disorder breathing subjects have more red

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.060
than the non-sleep disorder breathing subjects.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.380
Anyway, it's a nice little paper.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:15.410
It's on PubMed.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:21.480
Mostly what I'm going to talk about today
is principles of exploratory data analysis.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:27.310
If you want a lot of practical information
about exploratory data analysis, in other

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:32.320
words, if you want to know, how do I do exploratory
data analysis in R in specific or Python in

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.150
specific, or Sassin specific, or something
like that.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:40.040
Certainly the best resource for R, I would
say, is Roger Peng's Exploratory Data Analysis

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.040
with R book.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.100
It's just all about how do I do it, not why
do I do it, or what are some of the guiding

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:49.070
principles of how you do it, which is what
I'll talk about today.

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:51.000
He also has a Coursera course.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.460
I should make a small comment.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.220
It was actually Coursera that came up with
the idea of a specialization.

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.530
But we jumped on it rather quickly, to our
credit.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:03.340
And then, of course, the Bible of exploratory
data analysis is John Tukey's book, if you

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.780
can find a copy of that.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:12.740
But this book by Roger Peng, and his course,
are very good, practical how-to type books.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:19.120
And today, what I'm going to cover are things
that are really-- don't apply across however

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.590
you're doing exploratory data analysis, whether
you're doing it in Python, or R, or whatever.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.430
So let me just start by saying what exploratory
data analysis is.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:34.070
And if I'm going to define what exploratory
data analysis is, I should define what statistical

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:37.610
activities are not exploratory data analysis.

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:42.750
So the traditional dichotomy that people talk
about is to differentiate between Exploratory

00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:47.260
Data Analysis, EDA, and Confirmatory Data
Analysis, CDA.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:53.690
So exploratory data analysis focuses on discovery
and hypothesis generation, whereas confirmatory

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:58.850
data analysis tends to focus on hypothesis
confirmation.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:03.970
Exploratory data analysis still controls error
rates and performs uncertainty quantification,

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.580
but it tends to do that much more loosely.

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:11.760
Confirmatory data analysis tends to focus
on formal inference and prediction techniques.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.490
Both of them can use the same methods.

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:19.360
The difference is exploratory data analysis
tends to be more freeform and less structured,

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.750
and I liken it to improvisational jazz.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:27.410
And then CDA is a little bit more prescriptive,
protocolized and planned, and so I have a

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:28.920
picture of a symphony down there.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:36.130
So I think that differentiates the idea behind
the two techniques.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:41.160
I would say in general that data analysis
falls on a spectrum between the highly prescriptive

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:45.600
and formal
conformatory data analysis sort of ideal,

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.580
and that's probably most realized in the area
of highly regulated clinical trials.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.210
So where clinical trials tend to be highly
protocolized and regularized, especially in

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:56.700
drug development.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:03.340
And then there are some areas like high throughput
technological measurement areas, like genomics

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:08.919
tends to fall in this category, where things
are a little bit more exploratory in nature.

00:07:08.919 --> 00:07:14.210
And so I think things-- though typically most
analyses fall in some gray zone between the

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:15.210
two.

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:19.880
So I actually often think the EDA versus CDA
division is more useful conceptually than

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:21.550
practically.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:25.990
And then I would say maybe an alternative
dichotomy, if you really have to have a dichotomy,

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:31.540
is to think about whether or not you're using
strongly phrased scientific hypothesis to

00:07:31.540 --> 00:07:34.990
drive your research versus doing purely empirical
studies.

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.980
I think that's a more useful division.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:43.280
But I actually put some papers down here that
discuss some of these issues.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:47.560
For example, this paper by Kell and Oliver
that says, here's the evidence, now what's

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:48.580
the hypothesis?

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:54.590
And it talks about the roles of inductive
versus hypothesis-driven science in genomics.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:58.600
And then this great blog post by Simply Statistics,
where it says the keyword in data science

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.180
is not data, it's science.

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:09.250
Therefore obviously promoting this kind of
strong, hypothesisdriven research agenda.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:13.810
And then again, I haven't really defined EDA
or really gone into much about the specifics

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:20.580
of EDA, but I want to put one of the biggest
points that's generally brought up about EDA

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:27.440
versus CDA now is the idea of doing a lot
of exploratory analysis and then presenting

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:31.380
only the final step, as if that was the only
step that you did.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.750
And that particular process is called p-hacking
or data-- or phishing.

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:37.840
Or phishing expeditions, or that sort of thing.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.169
And it generally falls under the rubric of
the more you use your data for hypothesis

00:08:42.169 --> 00:08:46.869
generation and exploration, the harder it
gets to really control the error rates on

00:08:46.869 --> 00:08:49.889
the same data for some sort of final analysis.

00:08:49.889 --> 00:08:54.160
And I would say there's been a lot of research
in the past couple of years on the extent

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.399
and consequences of this problem.

00:08:56.399 --> 00:09:01.000
So probably most notable was Ioannidis' very
famous article that said, why most published

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.259
research findings are false.

00:09:02.259 --> 00:09:08.009
Then I found this other great article on the
extent and consequences of p-hacking in science,

00:09:08.009 --> 00:09:10.430
which I thought was also very interesting.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:16.350
And then Jeff Leek and Leah Jager, who are
here in the department with me, both really

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:18.829
great researchers.

00:09:18.829 --> 00:09:25.089
Really tried to put some numbers on this idea
of the rate of false positive findings in

00:09:25.089 --> 00:09:26.089
science.

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:29.339
And they gave an estimate of the false discovery
rate in the top medical literature.

00:09:29.339 --> 00:09:34.820
So I would suggest you read all this literature,
and maybe even the broader literature in this

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:39.980
area, if you're interested in this problem
on using the exploratory data analysis too

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:43.240
much, to the point where it biases your current
formatory results.

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:50.480
But that is a big warning about big-- a big
no-no is to do a lot of exploratory data analysis

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:54.399
and then present the final step as if it was
confirmatory.

00:09:54.399 --> 00:09:59.380
So now let me actually get to the topic of
exploratory data analysis.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:04.089
So I'm going to give-- this is a shout out
to Jeff Leek, who teaches exploratory data

00:10:04.089 --> 00:10:05.149
analysis in his class.

00:10:05.149 --> 00:10:07.459
I borrowed a lot of slides from him.

00:10:07.459 --> 00:10:11.730
He had this great breakdown of what he thinks
of as the steps in an exploratory data analysis,

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.839
and I think it's a really nice summary.

00:10:14.839 --> 00:10:17.009
And these are reading in the data.

00:10:17.009 --> 00:10:18.009
Figuring out the data.

00:10:18.009 --> 00:10:23.679
After you read it in, you need to figure out
what the columns are, that sort of thing.

00:10:23.679 --> 00:10:24.699
Pre-processing it.

00:10:24.699 --> 00:10:29.160
Looking at dimensions and making sure they
measure up, especially if you have more than

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:32.060
one dataset that you're trying to merge together.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:33.399
You have to look at its values.

00:10:33.399 --> 00:10:34.839
Make sure there's no weird values.

00:10:34.839 --> 00:10:38.709
You need to make tables and hunt for messed
up values.

00:10:38.709 --> 00:10:41.410
Figure out how missing data is coded.

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.949
NA stands for missing data.

00:10:42.949 --> 00:10:44.570
How missing data is coded.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:45.830
How much missing data is there?

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:47.339
Is missing data coded consistently?

00:10:47.339 --> 00:10:50.499
And then you want to do a lot of plots.

00:10:50.499 --> 00:10:54.209
Plots are the cornerstone of exploratory data
analysis.

00:10:54.209 --> 00:10:57.180
And then the final step says don't fool yourself.

00:10:57.180 --> 00:11:00.920
So today-- and I'll talk more about that--
but today, really, these are the things I'm

00:11:00.920 --> 00:11:02.529
going to focus on.

00:11:02.529 --> 00:11:08.139
And there's a lot of content in exploratory
data analysis, and a lot of content in these

00:11:08.139 --> 00:11:09.959
areas I'm not going to cover.

00:11:09.959 --> 00:11:13.670
But just in a one-hour talk, I certainly can't
get them all.

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:19.019
So I'm going to spend a little bit of time
on pre-processing, a little bit of time on

00:11:19.019 --> 00:11:24.899
not fooling yourself, and a lot of time on
some basic guidance on creating plots.

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:26.939
So let's talk about pre-processing.

00:11:26.939 --> 00:11:34.709
So we recently had a really wonderful talk
from Jenny Bryan, who is a very notable data

00:11:34.709 --> 00:11:40.540
scientist who works at UBC, but then also
just got hired by RStudio to be part of their

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.279
educational and development team.

00:11:43.279 --> 00:11:47.929
And there aren't a lot of great principals
in pre-processing.

00:11:47.929 --> 00:11:52.839
There's almost like-- it's almost like an
artistic endeavor, where you do it differently

00:11:52.839 --> 00:11:54.449
for every problem.

00:11:54.449 --> 00:11:59.610
And I think one of the things, if I'm thinking
broadly about what her basic research agenda

00:11:59.610 --> 00:12:04.480
is, is to really try and synthesize this process
into some general principles.

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:08.429
And one general principle, she said, which
I think is a very useful one, is to try and

00:12:08.429 --> 00:12:10.009
get everything into a rectangle.

00:12:10.009 --> 00:12:13.879
And she actually had this picture of a rectangle
with angel wings on it.

00:12:13.879 --> 00:12:18.319
And let me just expand on this a little bit.

00:12:18.319 --> 00:12:21.480
She really emphasized this idea that data
wrangling is work.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:26.759
So if you spend a day or two days getting
your data into a nice format, don't think

00:12:26.759 --> 00:12:29.129
of that as, well, I haven't even started working
yet.

00:12:29.129 --> 00:12:32.989
The reality is, you've done a lot of work,
and some of the most important work.

00:12:32.989 --> 00:12:38.670
It's like the most exciting part of building
a house is seeing it all come together and

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.540
getting a lot of the nice finishing touches.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:47.110
But without laying the concrete for a firm
foundation, the rest of the house is irrelevant.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:50.689
And so
no one likes laying the foundation, but everyone

00:12:50.689 --> 00:12:55.040
thinks it's probably one of the most valuable
parts of building a house.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:58.989
And I put that into this quote that would
say, no one has ever said, I really regret

00:12:58.989 --> 00:13:02.940
getting my data into such a wellorganized
and thought out format.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:06.499
So some basic things you want to remember
is to try and save your steps while you're

00:13:06.499 --> 00:13:07.499
doing it.

00:13:07.499 --> 00:13:11.750
Use a version control system, like Git, try
and engage in reproducible research as you're

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:13.160
doing this.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:16.600
And then her point was also try and get your
data into a rectangle.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:20.050
Name your columns with a sensible naming convention.

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:22.100
Use names that are amenable to software packages.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:26.920
So don't put spaces and quotation marks and
weird symbols and other things that aren't

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:29.730
useful for coding, in your names.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:36.600
Try and use capitalization and spacing in
your column names like you're a programmer.

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:37.600
No special features.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.449
If you're using a spreadsheet, don't put embedded
graphs in your basic data format.

00:13:41.449 --> 00:13:43.339
Just try and split that out.

00:13:43.339 --> 00:13:44.980
Split that process out.

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:49.399
Even if you're using Excel to perform your
data analysis, try and create copies of the

00:13:49.399 --> 00:13:57.600
dataset where you record how you created the
copy and create graphs and different sheets.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:02.040
Don't use numbers for missing values is one
that comes up for me a lot, where people code

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:07.179
missing values as 888, and then it gets read
into a software package that doesn't recognize

00:14:07.179 --> 00:14:08.399
that and treats it as a number.

00:14:08.399 --> 00:14:09.509
It messes up the analysis.

00:14:09.509 --> 00:14:12.179
And usually you can detect that very quickly.

00:14:12.179 --> 00:14:16.120
But if you don't, then it messes everything
up.

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:17.139
OK.

00:14:17.139 --> 00:14:21.379
And there some tools-- again, I'm not going
to spend a lot of this talk talking about

00:14:21.379 --> 00:14:22.379
tools.

00:14:22.379 --> 00:14:28.379
But in R, if you like to use R, there is a
grammar of data wrangling that's coming about.

00:14:28.379 --> 00:14:34.920
It's still under development, but tools like
dplyr and tidyr and purrr and stringr and

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:37.110
these things are really making it a lot easier.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:38.110
In R in specific.

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:44.610
Of course, you can do data wrangling in any
of the major analysis programs.

00:14:44.610 --> 00:14:46.639
Now let me talk about not fooling yourself.

00:14:46.639 --> 00:14:50.029
And the first thing I'm going to do when I
talk about not fooling yourself is give a

00:14:50.029 --> 00:14:54.750
couple of parables that go along with this.

00:14:54.750 --> 00:14:59.920
So the first is the famous one of the elephant
and the blind men.

00:14:59.920 --> 00:15:02.980
This picture was off of Wikipedia.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:05.059
And it's the story of the elephant and the
blind men.

00:15:05.059 --> 00:15:12.980
It's that six blind men are investigating
an elephant, and one of them touches its side

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:14.090
and says, it's a wall!

00:15:14.090 --> 00:15:16.939
And another one touches the trunk and says,
it's a snake!

00:15:16.939 --> 00:15:18.029
And another-- you get the picture.

00:15:18.029 --> 00:15:22.160
They all got a very incomplete picture of
the elephant.

00:15:22.160 --> 00:15:25.069
And this parable is used to illustrate several
points.

00:15:25.069 --> 00:15:34.160
But I think germane to our discussion is you're
really-- don't be like the blind man investigating

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:38.449
the elephant in that-- or at least acknowledge
that you were like that, in the sense that

00:15:38.449 --> 00:15:42.279
you only get to work with the data that you
get to see.

00:15:42.279 --> 00:15:49.149
Another thing that's very common is this kind
of bias that you get by finding something,

00:15:49.149 --> 00:15:51.540
finding a pattern, and then
pretending like it was what you were searching

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:52.720
for to begin with.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.869
And this is most often described as shooting
an arrow and painting a bull's eye around

00:15:56.869 --> 00:15:57.869
where it lands.

00:15:57.869 --> 00:16:01.720
I actually found online a company called the
Bullseye Painting Company.

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:06.899
So I think they don't actually paint bullseyes,
I think that bullseye is just their name.

00:16:06.899 --> 00:16:11.050
But at any rate, that's an important consideration
when you're doing exploratory data analysis

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:15.079
is coming up with a chance finding and then
pretending like it was something you were

00:16:15.079 --> 00:16:17.059
searching for all along.

00:16:17.059 --> 00:16:19.480
Then there's this famous Mutt and Jeff cartoon.

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:23.269
I got this from Quote Investigator.

00:16:23.269 --> 00:16:27.860
Where it's a drunk looking for his quarter
underneath a lamppost, and it says, I'm looking

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:29.189
for my quarter that I dropped.

00:16:29.189 --> 00:16:31.160
And the policeman says, did you drop it here?

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:33.740
And the drunk says, no I dropped it two blocks
down the street.

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:36.100
And then he says, well, why are you looking
for it here?

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.079
And the drunk says, because the light is better
here.

00:16:39.079 --> 00:16:43.699
And the idea is that you're looking at the
data that you have with the biases that it

00:16:43.699 --> 00:16:52.600
has, and we want to as little as possible
be like drunks looking for our change under

00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:54.490
a lamp post.

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:57.389
Looking where there's light rather than looking
where we need to be looking.

00:16:57.389 --> 00:17:01.739
And then of course the one that comes up the
most, that most people are aware of, is this

00:17:01.739 --> 00:17:03.459
problem of multiplicity.

00:17:03.459 --> 00:17:05.770
And this is a great XKCD comic book.

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:07.850
It's number 882.

00:17:07.850 --> 00:17:12.669
And in this comic book, someone says, jellybeans
cause acne.

00:17:12.669 --> 00:17:14.620
And another person says, scientists investigate.

00:17:14.620 --> 00:17:18.490
And the scientists look, and it says, we found
no link between jelly beans and acne.

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:21.809
Then someone says-- then the person says,
I hear that it's only a certain color.

00:17:21.809 --> 00:17:24.829
And then they start investigating all the
colors, and then basically, all these panels

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:26.980
are people investigating different colors.

00:17:26.980 --> 00:17:31.559
And then they find green does have an association,
and then there's a news article that says

00:17:31.559 --> 00:17:33.480
green jelly beans are linked to acne.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:38.700
Of course, what this is illustrating is that
if you keep looking for things in noise, you'll

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.270
eventually find them.

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:45.250
So some example common ways you can fool yourself
in exploratory data analysis.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:49.130
So these are some of the most common ways
this can come up.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.820
And I'll relate them to the parables.

00:17:51.820 --> 00:17:54.700
So one thing is just issues with the data
that you have.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:57.540
Can it even answer the questions that you're
trying to ask?

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:02.670
That's the parable of the elephant and the
drunk under the lamppost.

00:18:02.670 --> 00:18:05.110
Both discuss that point.

00:18:05.110 --> 00:18:10.270
Another thing is that even if you find a true
thing, those true things may not paint a complete

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:11.270
picture.

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:12.580
That's, of course, the story of the elephant.

00:18:12.580 --> 00:18:20.130
The confirmation bias, the idea that you pretend
like you discover things that you were just

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:24.760
looking for ahead of time, and you just confirmed
them, but ignore the evidence that refutes

00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:25.760
it.

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:27.730
That's like painting the bullseye.

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:31.980
False findings are always a problem, just
where you get chance associations.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:34.010
That's like the bullseye in multiplicity.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:38.580
And then of course the problem of multiplicity
is just repeatedly looking for things until

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:41.039
you find something.

00:18:41.039 --> 00:18:46.149
So these are all the ways that you can fool
yourself, and I think these problems are more

00:18:46.149 --> 00:18:51.070
pronounced in exploratory data analysis, because
we're not even pretending that we have some

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:54.549
sort of strict error rate control that we're
trying to go through.

00:18:54.549 --> 00:18:59.039
Rarely in exploratory data analysis do you
have a highly protocolized version of what

00:18:59.039 --> 00:19:01.789
you're doing.

00:19:01.789 --> 00:19:07.890
So now let me-- so that discusses ways in
which you can fool yourself.

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:13.980
Now, the rest of the discussion, I'm going
to spend entirely talking about interocular

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:17.789
content, or other words, plots.

00:19:17.789 --> 00:19:19.159
Things that hit you right between the eyes.

00:19:19.159 --> 00:19:27.090
And the idea is that plots are really the
cornerstone of exploratory data analysis.

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:31.860
And then plot-- there's something about visual
information that pictures really are often

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:37.290
worth a thousand words, and there's just something
about them that can really speak to us and

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:43.880
drive home points and help us discover things
that we-- just a table or a number or a written

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.620
summary just somehow lacks.

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:47.460
And I'll give a great example.

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:56.279
Probably the most famous graph in graph design
and exploratory data analysis history is this

00:19:56.279 --> 00:20:02.030
plot by Menard about the French invasion in
Russia in 1812.

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:06.170
And this is generally thought of as one of
the most information-rich graphs that you

00:20:06.170 --> 00:20:07.170
can find.

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:12.051
It's also just beautiful in and of itself.

00:20:12.051 --> 00:20:14.870
And this is the original graph, and then you
can see it's the same thing.

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:22.429
But here is a version I grabbed off of Wikipedia
that has the English translations on it.

00:20:22.429 --> 00:20:31.509
And so what this graph depicts is as you go
from left to right here, this is showing the

00:20:31.509 --> 00:20:33.980
French troops as they marched into Russia.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.620
Starting here, and then ending up in Moscow.

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.049
Down here, you actually see the temperature.

00:20:40.049 --> 00:20:41.279
OK?

00:20:41.279 --> 00:20:50.440
And the path actually does quite closely mirror
the geographic path that the troops took.

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:51.440
Right?

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:55.740
So this really does look like the path they
were taking.

00:20:55.740 --> 00:21:01.309
The width of the graph represents the number
of troops at that particular time.

00:21:01.309 --> 00:21:07.730
And you have the troops-- and these little
breakouts things are groups that broke off.

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:08.730
Right?

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:11.360
That tried to go a different direction.

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:15.529
And then the black line coming back this way
is the retreating troops.

00:21:15.529 --> 00:21:20.890
So this group, for example, broke off, and
then they retreated, and this is this retreating

00:21:20.890 --> 00:21:23.900
group, and this is this retreating group.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:24.900
OK?

00:21:24.900 --> 00:21:30.409
And what was interesting historically, what
happened was the French had a very strong

00:21:30.409 --> 00:21:31.409
troop.

00:21:31.409 --> 00:21:33.399
They started out with a collection of troops.

00:21:33.399 --> 00:21:36.070
They started out with 422,000.

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:37.070
OK?

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:42.090
And the Russians had a very harsh strategy.

00:21:42.090 --> 00:21:46.410
They were going to engage the French troops
and then retreat and

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:48.030
then engage them and then retreat.

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:53.889
And then as they retreated, they used these
scorched earth protocols, where they would

00:21:53.889 --> 00:21:58.210
burn all the fields.

00:21:58.210 --> 00:22:02.669
And as they retreated, the French troops would
have no way to resupply.

00:22:02.669 --> 00:22:09.250
And so their goal, the way the Russians were
going to win this campaign was just by basically

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:15.679
freezing and starving the French troops to
death with a war of attrition as the Russian

00:22:15.679 --> 00:22:19.580
troops just repeatedly fell back toward Moscow.

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:23.320
And you can just see with the width here,
the way this graph displays the death toll,

00:22:23.320 --> 00:22:24.750
it's just striking.

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:28.840
So it started out with 422,000, down to 100,000.

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:36.039
You can almost feel the cold and horror as
these troops are trying to cross this river.

00:22:36.039 --> 00:22:41.179
When they get to 100,000, when they hit Moscow,
and they get to retreat, and then you see

00:22:41.179 --> 00:22:43.580
down here, back at the beginning, 10,000 troops.

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:47.139
So a loss of over 400,000 troops in this march.

00:22:47.139 --> 00:22:56.929
Any rate, at same time, you can see that the
temperature along the bottom axis here, helping

00:22:56.929 --> 00:23:01.630
show when the temperature spikes caused large
troop losses.

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:07.519
Anyway, a really wonderful graph, and it's
just a highlight of how graphs can be used

00:23:07.519 --> 00:23:09.380
to just-- used beautifully.

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:14.380
At any rate, this is such a famous graph,
I thought I'd describe it.

00:23:14.380 --> 00:23:20.889
Here's another famous plot that really helps
us more easily drill down on why plots are

00:23:20.889 --> 00:23:22.270
inherently useful.

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:31.540
So Anscombe was a very famous statistician,
and he created a data set where the mean of

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:35.860
the x's, the mean of the y's, the correlation
between the x's and the y's, and hence the

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:39.769
r squared value and the slope of the regression
line.

00:23:39.769 --> 00:23:44.220
The standard deviation, the x, the standard
deviation-wise, these were all the same, matched

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:45.720
from these graphs.

00:23:45.720 --> 00:23:50.179
And so if you ran these in a regression model
or a correlation, or you did a basic summary,

00:23:50.179 --> 00:23:54.200
like mean of x, mean of y, standard deviation
x, standard deviation y, you would get the

00:23:54.200 --> 00:23:56.370
same answer for each of these four graphs.

00:23:56.370 --> 00:24:00.700
However, obviously, there's very different
stories being told by the four graphs.

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:04.790
This one looks like kind of just a regular
noisy regression relationship.

00:24:04.790 --> 00:24:08.539
This one clearly looks like a noise-free parabolic
curve.

00:24:08.539 --> 00:24:11.450
This one looks like a noise-free line with
one outlier.

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:16.919
This one has almost no variation in the x
variable except one point that's way outside

00:24:16.919 --> 00:24:18.770
of the data cloud.

00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:26.720
So in all of these cases, unless you had done
the graph, if you had just done the obvious

00:24:26.720 --> 00:24:30.659
summaries, you would miss this incredibly
different story from each of these four datasets.

00:24:30.659 --> 00:24:34.400
And he did this exactly to make it as striking
as possible.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:39.399
Another great example is Len Stefanski, who's
at NC State.

00:24:39.399 --> 00:24:41.049
Likes to trick his students.

00:24:41.049 --> 00:24:45.789
And he creates these settings where you have
a regression model.

00:24:45.789 --> 00:24:49.659
And if you look, it looks like the variables
just follow a nice regression model.

00:24:49.659 --> 00:24:51.509
There are some significant p-values.

00:24:51.509 --> 00:24:55.901
And if you fail to do a residual plot, you
could just run through the analysis, figure

00:24:55.901 --> 00:25:00.500
out what variables might be necessary, and
see nothing in particular.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:05.690
But what happens is if you plot your predictive
versus your residual values for the model

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:09.940
he's telling people to start with, you actually
get a picture of Bob Dylan in the residuals.

00:25:09.940 --> 00:25:15.179
And he's written some algorithms that show
how you can create whatever picture that you

00:25:15.179 --> 00:25:16.950
want in the residuals.

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:20.690
So he can tell whether his students did the
plot or not, because they would obviously

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:25.440
comment, hey, there was a picture of Bob Dylan
in my residuals.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:32.870
So I hope that illustrates some of the important
reasons of why we need to do plots.

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:36.909
And then I'm basically going to spend the
rest of the lecture talking about ways in

00:25:36.909 --> 00:25:40.669
which we can improve our plots or some pitfalls
to avoid.

00:25:40.669 --> 00:25:41.669
OK?

00:25:41.669 --> 00:25:48.820
So as some general principles, one is by Tufte,
which is, I think, a really great contribution

00:25:48.820 --> 00:25:53.140
to the plotting literature, which is to maximize
the data to ink ratios.

00:25:53.140 --> 00:25:56.470
So if you take-- this slide is-- these slides
are from Karl Broman.

00:25:56.470 --> 00:25:59.480
I don't see it noted on here, but I'll add
that.

00:25:59.480 --> 00:26:05.660
Karl Broman is a faculty member at Wisconsin
who's a real expert in exploratory data analysis.

00:26:05.660 --> 00:26:09.710
So here you have this great plot, where you
have a response plotted by the treatment versus

00:26:09.710 --> 00:26:10.710
control.

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:14.190
It has incredible data to ink ratio, because
it displays all of the data with very little

00:26:14.190 --> 00:26:18.580
ink, and then it has this nice mean plus to
standard deviation confidence intervals for

00:26:18.580 --> 00:26:19.610
the two groups.

00:26:19.610 --> 00:26:25.850
An incredible amount of information recreates
the whole dataset in one simple plot.

00:26:25.850 --> 00:26:28.960
In contrast, you could do this plot as a bar
chart.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:29.960
Two bar charts.

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:34.070
And then look at the amount of ink you're
using, effectively to display two numbers;

00:26:34.070 --> 00:26:37.110
the mean for the treatment, and the mean for
the control.

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:42.530
Just the loss of information going from left
to right in these two plots is simply incredible.

00:26:42.530 --> 00:26:48.860
But also the increase in the amount of just
toner from your cartridge that you need to

00:26:48.860 --> 00:26:52.419
get this second plot to display two numbers
that you could have just put in a paper or

00:26:52.419 --> 00:26:56.110
whatever anyway is almost ludicrous.

00:26:56.110 --> 00:27:00.970
So that's the principle that Tufte is trying
to elucidate, is try to create plots like

00:27:00.970 --> 00:27:05.120
this one on the left, where you display the
data as much as you can.

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:09.389
And when in doubt, try to devote ink to data.

00:27:09.389 --> 00:27:15.230
Devote-- I think probably a useful way to
put it would be information to ink ratio.

00:27:15.230 --> 00:27:19.830
You want every bit of ink you're using to
display important information.

00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:21.840
This gray background isn't adding anything.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:24.539
Most of these lines aren't adding anything
on the right plot.

00:27:24.539 --> 00:27:28.039
The purple-- this is not a very informative
plot.

00:27:28.039 --> 00:27:31.230
It's just basically displaying two numbers.

00:27:31.230 --> 00:27:35.960
Another general principle is don't use 3-D.
So here we've taken that same plot, and we've

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:40.710
basically ruined it even further by adding
3-D. You have this optical illusion of this

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:44.600
corner here that doesn't add anything either.

00:27:44.600 --> 00:27:49.029
And just to really highlight the principle
of why you shouldn't use 3-D, we can basically

00:27:49.029 --> 00:27:53.110
take this plot and remove
all information by the angle at which we look

00:27:53.110 --> 00:27:54.110
at it.

00:27:54.110 --> 00:27:59.580
So and this just is to bring it to the point
of ludicrousness, where you're looking straight

00:27:59.580 --> 00:28:00.580
down at it.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.999
And of course now, we don't even display our
two numbers.

00:28:02.999 --> 00:28:06.600
This is just-- we've removed all information
content from our graph.

00:28:06.600 --> 00:28:11.240
But that's also showing that as you take this
3-D plot and rotate it a little bit, you're

00:28:11.240 --> 00:28:12.350
losing information.

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:16.730
Now, I work in brain imaging, and very often
we use 3-D because the brain is a three-dimensional

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:19.799
object, and we're using it very carefully
to try and display information.

00:28:19.799 --> 00:28:27.009
But I think you can say a general principle
is that for ordinary plots, don't use 3-D.

00:28:27.009 --> 00:28:28.580
Another thing is logging.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:37.299
So taking the natural log or a log base 2
or a log base 10 can be crucial if the scale--

00:28:37.299 --> 00:28:39.350
if orders of magnitude are important.

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:43.750
So here's an example that Karl came up with
in a genomic setting.

00:28:43.750 --> 00:28:47.700
You can see on the right hand side of this
plot, it's log base 10.

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:52.669
And you see this interesting variation in
separation between the groups, with a lot

00:28:52.669 --> 00:28:53.669
of information.

00:28:53.669 --> 00:28:58.070
Here's the same data set unlogged, and you
see, basically, it looks like everything is

00:28:58.070 --> 00:28:59.070
0.

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:04.380
So if it is the case that orders of magnitude
are important, take for example an obvious

00:29:04.380 --> 00:29:06.290
setting, like astronomical distances.

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:11.090
You obviously care more there about orders
of magnitude than anything else.

00:29:11.090 --> 00:29:15.410
If orders of magnitude are important, then
if you look at this plot in the way on the

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:19.370
right, you've lost all the relevant information
by not taking logs.

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:23.389
So at any rate, taking logs is often a very
important thing to do.

00:29:23.389 --> 00:29:29.240
Here's another example Karl came up with,
and this is a mean difference plot on the

00:29:29.240 --> 00:29:30.360
log scale.

00:29:30.360 --> 00:29:37.830
So it's the log of the ratio of two gene expression
levels, and then it's the average of the two

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:38.830
gene expression levels.

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:43.090
And you see all this interesting variation
on this plot.

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:45.379
Also what this does is it takes the scatterplot.

00:29:45.379 --> 00:29:50.890
You might think, well, I could log expression
level 1 by log expression level 2 in a scatterplot.

00:29:50.890 --> 00:29:56.179
This basically does the same thing but rotates
it 45 degrees to get rid of the unnecessary

00:29:56.179 --> 00:29:57.179
blank space.

00:29:57.179 --> 00:30:03.580
And you could see interesting patterns, such
as the variance of the difference decreasing

00:30:03.580 --> 00:30:06.730
by the increase in the average log expression
rate.

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:09.970
But you can also see the bulk of the data.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:11.889
It's spread out in a nice way.

00:30:11.889 --> 00:30:16.889
If, for example, you were to plot this unlogged,
as a scatterplot, first of all, you have all

00:30:16.889 --> 00:30:21.539
this unnecessary white space above and below
the collection of data.

00:30:21.539 --> 00:30:26.789
But as Karl points out, 99% of the data is
below this red line.

00:30:26.789 --> 00:30:27.789
OK?

00:30:27.789 --> 00:30:31.259
So you might look at this plot and come to
some conclusions, but you're really only looking

00:30:31.259 --> 00:30:33.980
at 1% of the data.

00:30:33.980 --> 00:30:35.590
OK.

00:30:35.590 --> 00:30:46.129
So now the last thing I'd like to talk about--
I have about maybe 15 minutes left, is the

00:30:46.129 --> 00:30:47.440
psychology of plots.

00:30:47.440 --> 00:30:49.440
There's a science of plots.

00:30:49.440 --> 00:30:55.280
So let me just talk about for exploratory
plots, what are some general characteristics?

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:56.879
So usually you're making them quickly.

00:30:56.879 --> 00:30:59.240
You're not finalizing.

00:30:59.240 --> 00:31:01.690
So I'm not going to talk about design today.

00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:05.779
There could be an aspect of once you've got
a plot that conveys all the information that

00:31:05.779 --> 00:31:11.510
you want, how do you get it into a super nice
design for a reader for publication.

00:31:11.510 --> 00:31:15.120
OK, that's different than what goes on in
exploratory plots.

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:21.529
The plots are usually made for you or your
team as you're going through the data.

00:31:21.529 --> 00:31:23.950
So the goal is for personal understanding.

00:31:23.950 --> 00:31:25.640
And a large number of plots are made.

00:31:25.640 --> 00:31:26.720
They're made quickly.

00:31:26.720 --> 00:31:32.159
Generally, it is worth spending some time
labeling your axes.

00:31:32.159 --> 00:31:38.809
So if you can not only have your axes labeled,
but also have the units of the measurement

00:31:38.809 --> 00:31:40.580
on the axis, that is generally better.

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:44.440
But also the axis and the tick marks and things
like that are usually cleaned up, because

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:50.320
otherwise we'll see that that's a really easy
way to make plots difficult to understand

00:31:50.320 --> 00:31:53.380
is to not have spent any time on your axes.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:56.470
And then colors and size are primarily used
for information.

00:31:56.470 --> 00:31:59.490
Like I said, you're not spending-- in exploratory
data analysis, you're not spending a lot of

00:31:59.490 --> 00:32:05.299
time on final colors and things like that,
that are used to make it look nice for presentation.

00:32:05.299 --> 00:32:09.669
You're spending more time on information.

00:32:09.669 --> 00:32:16.879
And so especially for plotting, there is a
not terribly well developed, but at least

00:32:16.879 --> 00:32:23.820
a nice history and some great research in
what I would call a theory of EDA.

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:31.200
And what I mean by theory of EDA, I don't
actually mean the mathematical theory that

00:32:31.200 --> 00:32:36.100
underlies some characteristics of plots.

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:39.249
That exists, of course, and that's super welldeveloped.

00:32:39.249 --> 00:32:43.030
But what I'm talking about is the psychological
part of EDA.

00:32:43.030 --> 00:32:45.390
How we perceive plots.

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:50.299
And it's unfortunately true that we're designed
to find patterns even when there are aren't

00:32:50.299 --> 00:32:52.039
any.

00:32:52.039 --> 00:32:56.700
And our visual perception is then biased by
this humanness.

00:32:56.700 --> 00:33:02.179
And so again, the goal in EDA, just like we
discussed earlier, is not to fool yourself.

00:33:02.179 --> 00:33:11.350
And the real pioneer in this field is Bill
Cleveland, at least in the field of statistics.

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:14.809
I think it's a much bigger research area now.

00:33:14.809 --> 00:33:17.250
But in the field of statistics, he was a real
pioneer of it.

00:33:17.250 --> 00:33:19.759
So I'm going to talk about some of his early
work.

00:33:19.759 --> 00:33:27.030
But just to get us in the mood, let me show
you some slides where we see some optical

00:33:27.030 --> 00:33:32.200
illusions which could very realistically occur.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:36.250
Something like this could very realistically
occur in an exploratory data analysis.

00:33:36.250 --> 00:33:39.999
So in this optical illusion, these two middle
points are of the same size.

00:33:39.999 --> 00:33:48.429
But of course, because of the size and distance
of the surrounding points, we perceive them

00:33:48.429 --> 00:33:52.679
to be different in size, typically thinking
this one is smaller.

00:33:52.679 --> 00:34:00.369
This is some unintended framing that's happening.

00:34:00.369 --> 00:34:01.480
So that's one example.

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:04.649
This one drives me nuts.

00:34:04.649 --> 00:34:10.170
But you can load up this image into Photoshop
or whatever and check it yourself.

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:17.490
But the a and b squares in this plot are actually
the same tone of gray, which I find amazing.

00:34:17.490 --> 00:34:24.860
So the surrounding colors around-- and this
is achieved by this shadowing effect.

00:34:24.860 --> 00:34:34.030
But again, the idea is that even if you're
trying to compare something in a plot by tone,

00:34:34.030 --> 00:34:45.330
we can actually have instances where our perception
is very much so off with respect to tone.

00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:50.460
And the optical illusions just hone in on
this and make it as bad as it can possibly

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:54.160
be to highlight this principle.

00:34:54.160 --> 00:35:00.750
So another important point, just to return
to this point about multiplicity and testing

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:07.570
things until you find something that's true,
Hadley Wickham actually does exactly formalize

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:09.790
this concept.

00:35:09.790 --> 00:35:12.680
And there's a link down there.

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:15.550
Where you take, for example, a dataset.

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:18.040
Let's say this middle one is the real data.

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:22.830
He actually then permutes and plots.

00:35:22.830 --> 00:35:27.840
Some examples of the permuted dataset, so
that you can visually perform a hypothesis

00:35:27.840 --> 00:35:28.840
test.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:33.390
So he's formalizing this idea of a hypothesis
test done on data.

00:35:33.390 --> 00:35:38.090
However, this point illustrates to us that
whenever we do a plot, whenever we make a

00:35:38.090 --> 00:35:42.960
decision and then do another plot and make
a decision and do another plot, these are

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:45.420
informal hypothesis tests.

00:35:45.420 --> 00:35:48.040
These are informal models that we're fitting.

00:35:48.040 --> 00:35:52.630
And so all of the problems that exist that
we talk about with formal models, informal

00:35:52.630 --> 00:35:58.360
hypothesis tests get brought into this process,
just in a fuzzy way.

00:35:58.360 --> 00:35:59.360
OK.

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:04.790
Now, let me talk about Cleveland's work, because
it's so fundamental.

00:36:04.790 --> 00:36:12.250
And so what he starts with in this work, this
paper, where I have the link down here, is

00:36:12.250 --> 00:36:16.870
really-- the Journal of the American Statistical
Association paper is really the foundation

00:36:16.870 --> 00:36:20.140
of this work and is really just a fundamental
paper in this area.

00:36:20.140 --> 00:36:28.590
And one thing he brings up is this idea of
the DNA of perceptual tasks.

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:30.380
These perceptual units.

00:36:30.380 --> 00:36:35.140
And he basically says what we're going to
do is try and break down graph characteristics

00:36:35.140 --> 00:36:36.970
and do these perceptual units.

00:36:36.970 --> 00:36:42.490
So contracting lengths is an example of a
perception task.

00:36:42.490 --> 00:36:45.450
Comparing angles is an example of a perception
task.

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:50.891
Comparing direction and area and volume, curvature,
shading, position on a common scale, position

00:36:50.891 --> 00:36:52.290
on nonaligned scale.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:54.000
These are all perception tasks.

00:36:54.000 --> 00:37:00.470
And the argument he said is well, we can figure
out how people do-- we can isolate perception

00:37:00.470 --> 00:37:06.130
tasks, test how people do on them, and that
will back inform what kinds of graphs we should

00:37:06.130 --> 00:37:11.660
be creating.

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:21.270
So take this experiment he did, where he looked
at several different types of specific position

00:37:21.270 --> 00:37:23.630
and length type perception tasks.

00:37:23.630 --> 00:37:29.190
So in all of these cases, the people were
comparing trying to get an idea at the ratio

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:35.950
of the length of the two dotted bars.

00:37:35.950 --> 00:37:40.240
And they have type 1, where they are right
next to each other, another type where they're

00:37:40.240 --> 00:37:44.570
separated, but they're on the same scale,
in the lowest box.

00:37:44.570 --> 00:37:48.740
You know, this one, they're separated into
histograms.

00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:52.040
This one they're the top box separated.

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:54.360
And this one,
they're right on top of these others.

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:59.440
And then he called this the position and length
experiments, because some of these are varying

00:37:59.440 --> 00:38:03.050
position, and some of these are varying lengths
comparisons.

00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:07.330
And when he looks at this, we can see that
there are certain kinds of things-- this is

00:38:07.330 --> 00:38:12.880
the log absolute difference between the actual
true ratio and what the people he was testing,

00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:14.230
what they thought it was.

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:19.420
So he saw that certain positions were doing
better than other ones.

00:38:19.420 --> 00:38:22.460
And you can guess how it would work.

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:26.880
On this type 5, the length one, that's this
one, where we're comparing two things that

00:38:26.880 --> 00:38:28.700
are not next to each other.

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:29.700
Right?

00:38:29.700 --> 00:38:33.530
It's very hard to figure out the relationship
of these two things, because they're not directly

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:34.530
comparable.

00:38:34.530 --> 00:38:37.800
But these two things, type 1, we're doing
quite well at when they're right next to each

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:41.840
other and on a common-compared with a common
axis.

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:43.070
But he could quantify that.

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:48.460
And then he looked at several different perceptual
units.

00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:54.930
As an example, he also looked at volume, ratios
of volumes, by comparing things like positions

00:38:54.930 --> 00:38:55.930
or angles.

00:38:55.930 --> 00:38:59.550
So here would be something like a pie chart,
where we're comparing angles.

00:38:59.550 --> 00:39:03.130
If you're comparing the volumes of two things,
you're really making that comparison by virtue

00:39:03.130 --> 00:39:10.370
of the angle of the slice, whereas the bar--
he might consider comparing it with the position

00:39:10.370 --> 00:39:11.840
of the bars.

00:39:11.840 --> 00:39:18.690
And what they found was one thing is that
these angle comparisons were quite terrible.

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:22.670
The log absolute difference for the angle
comparisons was quite terrible.

00:39:22.670 --> 00:39:27.780
Another thing, one of the worst examples,
was if you had to compare the ratio of two

00:39:27.780 --> 00:39:28.780
slopes.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:36.650
And they looked at whether-- different ways
of displaying the-- different ways of displaying

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:37.650
the two slopes.

00:39:37.650 --> 00:39:42.330
So you want to compare the slope from A to
B to the slope from B to C. You want to estimate

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:43.330
that ratio.

00:39:43.330 --> 00:39:45.510
And of course, if you squash it in, it gets
harder to do.

00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:51.130
If you display it vertically rather than horizontally,
it gets harder to do.

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:52.490
And we're very bad at doing this.

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:56.190
But remember, we often make plots where we're
plotting the slopes of two groups.

00:39:56.190 --> 00:40:00.950
And intrinsically, we're asking the reader
of the plot to perform this calculation.

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:04.530
And what this research is showing is how much
you squash the plot, how much you stretch

00:40:04.530 --> 00:40:10.500
it, or even this task to begin with, is actually
quite difficult for people.

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:15.510
Another interesting-- and this is a separate
paper-- another interesting idea was that

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:18.550
the scale matters quite a bit, of the plot.

00:40:18.550 --> 00:40:24.550
So as an example, they showed people scatter
plots with the same correlation and found

00:40:24.550 --> 00:40:28.980
people reported dramatically different correlations,
depending on what they varied.

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:35.340
So here they might show things that have roughly
similar correlations, but when they zoom out,

00:40:35.340 --> 00:40:40.370
people actually estimate, if they visually
estimate the correlation, they say that it's

00:40:40.370 --> 00:40:43.030
higher when you zoom out.

00:40:43.030 --> 00:40:44.030
OK?

00:40:44.030 --> 00:40:50.250
So again, our perception of correlation is
dependent on the arbitrary scale at which

00:40:50.250 --> 00:40:54.030
we choose to display the data.

00:40:54.030 --> 00:40:58.130
Here's the result of this perception task,
right?

00:40:58.130 --> 00:41:01.950
So of course, if there's zero correlation,
people seem to be doing fine, and if there's

00:41:01.950 --> 00:41:05.030
a 100% correlation, people seem to be doing
fine.

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:08.770
But here are these-- plots the size of the
circle represents the variability at that

00:41:08.770 --> 00:41:09.770
point.

00:41:09.770 --> 00:41:16.480
You can see at around correlations, around
0.6, 0.5, 0.7 or so, people get the worst.

00:41:16.480 --> 00:41:21.150
They're the furthest away from what the actual
correlation was.

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:26.030
These two other lines-- I happened to reread
this paper last night, just to say this--

00:41:26.030 --> 00:41:30.740
are they were trying to figure out what were
the best geographic models.

00:41:30.740 --> 00:41:35.270
So they think this is a model for what people
are actually visually modeling, rather than

00:41:35.270 --> 00:41:39.720
this, which is closer to the truth.

00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:46.220
So they came up with theories of the geography
that they think people might actually be operating

00:41:46.220 --> 00:41:47.220
with.

00:41:47.220 --> 00:41:50.580
A good theory of the geography that people
appear not to be operating with, and a bad

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:54.520
theory of the geography that appears to not
be exactly go through the data, but appears

00:41:54.520 --> 00:42:00.150
to more closely correlate with what people
are doing.

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:05.170
Another great paper was by Jeff Leek, who
does some research in this area using our

00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:07.510
Coursera classes.

00:42:07.510 --> 00:42:15.420
And he actually had students try the experiment
of whether or not they could ascertain significance

00:42:15.420 --> 00:42:19.720
from a plot, of a correlation from a plot,
just by looking at scatter plot.

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:23.280
And one interesting thing they found was that
people could not do these kinds of tasks,

00:42:23.280 --> 00:42:27.070
but you could train them so that they were
able to do it.

00:42:27.070 --> 00:42:34.650
And they broke it down by different categories
of things that they could change.

00:42:34.650 --> 00:42:39.850
They broke down the accuracy by different
ways in which they-- they changed the way

00:42:39.850 --> 00:42:45.670
the plot was displayed, axis scale, whether
or not they put a low s-curve in there.

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:47.770
The smaller end versus larger end.

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:49.440
And showed versus accuracy.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:55.450
But an interesting component of this article
was A, that they were able to break down the

00:42:55.450 --> 00:43:01.940
various components that impacted whether or
not people were able to surmise these p-values.

00:43:01.940 --> 00:43:08.310
But then They also saw this training effect.

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:10.180
So some basic summaries.

00:43:10.180 --> 00:43:13.070
Whenever possible, use common scales.

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:17.471
One of the perception results was when you
mess up the scales, when you have two plots

00:43:17.471 --> 00:43:21.570
right next to each other, and one of them
is on one scale, and the other one's on another

00:43:21.570 --> 00:43:28.200
scale, that the comparison-- that translation,
mentally, is quite difficult for people.

00:43:28.200 --> 00:43:37.350
So when possible, use position comparisons,
things that are on the same scale, just measuring

00:43:37.350 --> 00:43:42.110
where they are relative to that scale, basically
asking where two things are when they fall

00:43:42.110 --> 00:43:43.170
on a ruler.

00:43:43.170 --> 00:43:47.590
Those are the best things that people are
the best at.

00:43:47.590 --> 00:43:51.360
One of the things they were the worst at was
angle comparisons.

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:53.270
So they're very hard for people to do.

00:43:53.270 --> 00:43:58.250
And a consequence of this is it basically
says that people are no good at interpreting

00:43:58.250 --> 00:44:01.260
pie charts.

00:44:01.260 --> 00:44:06.880
Things like we mentioned earlier, adding a
third dimension, generally doesn't add much,

00:44:06.880 --> 00:44:11.100
but also decreases from
people's ability to perceive things.

00:44:11.100 --> 00:44:16.850
And then again, this point that I've raised
up several times during this lecture is do

00:44:16.850 --> 00:44:19.520
not fool yourself about significance.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:22.110
And I think that I got the slide from Jeff
also.

00:44:22.110 --> 00:44:25.410
He makes an important point about saying in
either direction.

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:30.520
So we've talked a lot about not fooling ourselves
in the terms of not making false positives,

00:44:30.520 --> 00:44:33.670
but you don't want to then go on the other
way.

00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:38.500
You can avoid all false positives by saying
everything's junk, nothing is ever significant.

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:41.310
And you don't want to head in that direction
either way.

00:44:41.310 --> 00:44:46.950
You want a good compass to guide you.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:48.270
I just want to get some acknowledgement.

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:53.490
So I got a lot of these slides from Jeff Leek
and Karl Broman.

00:44:53.490 --> 00:44:59.970
And then Jenny Bryan, Genevera Allen, I got
some stuff from XKCD, Wikipedia, Len Stefanski,

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:01.490
and Rstudio.

00:45:01.490 --> 00:45:06.570
And then I think we-- I hit exactly 12:45,
so I saved some time for questions if there

00:45:06.570 --> 00:45:07.570
are any.

00:45:07.570 --> 00:45:08.680
Thank you, Brian.

00:45:08.680 --> 00:45:15.800
So if there are any questions, please type
them into the question box.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:22.680
Everyone's muted, so they can't actually ask
them, to keep all 200 people from asking at

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:23.680
once.

00:45:23.680 --> 00:45:28.460
But while we're waiting for more to show up
in the question box, I have one to ask you,

00:45:28.460 --> 00:45:37.980
and that's whether you have an example of
where a lack of preprocessing or lack of looking

00:45:37.980 --> 00:45:44.300
at your data ahead of time has really lead
you astray and really messed up your analysis

00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:46.700
later on.

00:45:46.700 --> 00:45:56.570
Well, I think, certainly anyone who teaches
a statistics class can give you an instance

00:45:56.570 --> 00:46:08.200
of when students have written reports, for
example, that when they don't look at values,

00:46:08.200 --> 00:46:09.310
they get messed up results.

00:46:09.310 --> 00:46:14.930
So most teachers will do something like spike
in some crazy values that will mess up everyone's

00:46:14.930 --> 00:46:18.350
results and try and teach people that lesson
early.

00:46:18.350 --> 00:46:23.740
But I think that lesson isn't-- it's very
hard to make that lesson sticky, because I've

00:46:23.740 --> 00:46:31.220
certainly had many times in my life where
I've gone through a full analysis, thought

00:46:31.220 --> 00:46:38.810
I'd found something super interesting, started
to write it up to share with my collaborators,

00:46:38.810 --> 00:46:44.110
and then found after-- maybe on the way to
talk to them, realized, oh, no.

00:46:44.110 --> 00:46:49.240
The direction of the effect is the exact opposite
of the direction that I thought it was going

00:46:49.240 --> 00:46:53.070
to be and that science would dictate.

00:46:53.070 --> 00:47:00.110
And then when I go back, I realize it's always
one of these sort of errors that there was

00:47:00.110 --> 00:47:06.860
some error in preprocessing, there were some
missing data that were coded as 888 or 999

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:18.640
that I didn't catch, or there was some errant
values, like someone who was 200 pounds being

00:47:18.640 --> 00:47:22.730
put in as 2,000 pounds, or something like
that.

00:47:22.730 --> 00:47:29.460
And that also reminds us not only to do plots,
but also check-- there's diagnostics like

00:47:29.460 --> 00:47:35.980
DFFITS and DFBETAS and things like that that
really help us, in our models, diagnose these

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:37.060
errant values.

00:47:37.060 --> 00:47:39.690
And it's always worthwhile to do those things.

00:47:39.690 --> 00:47:45.530
So I mean, I can say pretty much any time
I've ever failed to do the common steps of

00:47:45.530 --> 00:47:51.941
doing a lot of plots, checking my regression
diagnostics, every time I don't do that, I

00:47:51.941 --> 00:47:53.810
always wind up with something screwy.

00:47:53.810 --> 00:47:58.230
And then it just depends on how far I take
it before I have to go back and check it.

00:47:58.230 --> 00:48:01.600
Well, that's very good.

00:48:01.600 --> 00:48:08.190
That's a very good lesson for all of us to
learn is to spend time doing that first.

00:48:08.190 --> 00:48:13.340
But what about-- do you have a sense of what
percentage of your time you spend doing the

00:48:13.340 --> 00:48:17.730
exploratory analysis versus a confirmatory
analysis?

00:48:17.730 --> 00:48:24.380
I think that very much so depends on what
branch of science that you live in.

00:48:24.380 --> 00:48:28.630
I think I live in the discovery science world.

00:48:28.630 --> 00:48:39.650
So when I work on FMRI and MRI, much of my
analysis is really on this discovery site

00:48:39.650 --> 00:48:41.500
science.

00:48:41.500 --> 00:48:47.260
And in that setting, I think a lot more of
my analysis could be described as exploratory,

00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:49.540
as more toward the exploratory end.

00:48:49.540 --> 00:48:55.150
So then I would say, for many of those projects,
I would say, a huge chunk of it is exploratory.

00:48:55.150 --> 00:48:57.950
80% or something like that.

00:48:57.950 --> 00:49:02.600
Now there are other settings I work in where
this setting is more mature.

00:49:02.600 --> 00:49:04.440
And take an Alzheimer's disease.

00:49:04.440 --> 00:49:08.950
I think the setting, because there are so
many people working in Alzheimer's disease,

00:49:08.950 --> 00:49:12.950
we have a better scientific compass to lead
us.

00:49:12.950 --> 00:49:19.360
And in those settings, we tend to go into
the analysis with more directed hypotheses.

00:49:19.360 --> 00:49:25.360
And then I spend a lot less time-- we tend
to be able to come up with a nice frozen dataset

00:49:25.360 --> 00:49:28.920
that really is exactly what we're interested
in.

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:33.320
More often, in other cases, it seems like
we start out with a dataset that we're interested

00:49:33.320 --> 00:49:34.320
in.

00:49:34.320 --> 00:49:37.010
We find some interesting things and then find
that we didn't process the data in the way

00:49:37.010 --> 00:49:41.280
that was necessary to answer now these new
questions that arose, and then we have to

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:44.160
reprocess.

00:49:44.160 --> 00:49:50.200
And I certainly would think that someone who
worked in clinical trials would spend a little

00:49:50.200 --> 00:49:53.340
bit less time on data pre-processing.

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:54.660
But I don't know.

00:49:54.660 --> 00:50:00.020
That would be an interesting empirical study,
would be to get, actually, numbers about it.

00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:03.630
Because I'm sure what we perceive the amount
of time we spend on data pre-processing is

00:50:03.630 --> 00:50:06.430
different than what it actually is.

00:50:06.430 --> 00:50:08.570
Yeah, I bet.

00:50:08.570 --> 00:50:11.930
So we have a couple of questions in the question
box.

00:50:11.930 --> 00:50:14.380
The first one is about lasagna plots.

00:50:14.380 --> 00:50:17.180
Coming back to the very beginning.

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:22.870
And whether lasagna plots violate your data
to in ratio.

00:50:22.870 --> 00:50:24.520
They do have a lot of ink.

00:50:24.520 --> 00:50:25.670
That is true.

00:50:25.670 --> 00:50:28.960
But on the other hand, they are displaying
the full data set.

00:50:28.960 --> 00:50:33.150
So the numerator and the denominator is high
in that case.

00:50:33.150 --> 00:50:38.960
So I don't know if they have a great data
to ink ratio, but they do have at least--

00:50:38.960 --> 00:50:41.700
in our estimation-- a tolerable one.

00:50:41.700 --> 00:50:49.520
Especially because they tend to take large
datasets, and they tend to-- they basically

00:50:49.520 --> 00:50:52.100
redisplay the whole dataset.

00:50:52.100 --> 00:50:58.380
The key with lasagna plots is not so much
the original plot, because that's interesting

00:50:58.380 --> 00:50:59.380
in and of itself.

00:50:59.380 --> 00:51:03.800
And if you're lucky, like in the sleep experiment,
where there's some obvious missing data pattern

00:51:03.800 --> 00:51:10.110
that, for whatever the reason, was common
across subjects, that's great.

00:51:10.110 --> 00:51:14.780
But probably the key to the lasagna plots
is some sort of sorting or organizing your

00:51:14.780 --> 00:51:17.590
rows, where you can try to do to detect patterns.

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:18.590
But anyway, yeah.

00:51:18.590 --> 00:51:24.990
The point is well taken that yeah, they do
use a lot of ink.

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:26.260
OK.

00:51:26.260 --> 00:51:32.870
And then the next question is about any suggestions
that you might have for software to do EDA.

00:51:32.870 --> 00:51:39.930
Do you have any preferences on software, or
can you just give some advice about what are

00:51:39.930 --> 00:51:41.880
some things people might use?

00:51:41.880 --> 00:51:42.940
Yes.

00:51:42.940 --> 00:51:48.580
So I use R. And then-- but I've been using
R for a long time.

00:51:48.580 --> 00:51:54.920
So I use Base R, which is just R's default
plotting stuff.

00:51:54.920 --> 00:52:04.670
Since then, in R, there's been a revolution
with this package called ggplot.

00:52:04.670 --> 00:52:07.940
And ggplot, the ggplot stands for grammar
of graphics.

00:52:07.940 --> 00:52:14.880
And this was a theory that was put forward
on-- worked on by starting back-- I think

00:52:14.880 --> 00:52:20.890
it dates back to Cleveland and other work
at Bell Labs and then goes all the way forward

00:52:20.890 --> 00:52:23.120
to Hadley Wickham's work on it, and several
other people.

00:52:23.120 --> 00:52:28.500
And then I forget who the actual inventor
of the term and the concepts of grammar of

00:52:28.500 --> 00:52:29.500
graphics.

00:52:29.500 --> 00:52:36.290
Any rate, this led to a system that is then
operationalized in the R package, ggplot,

00:52:36.290 --> 00:52:38.910
which is, I think, one of the most popular
R packages.

00:52:38.910 --> 00:52:45.090
And if you get used to ggplot, people seem
to absolutely love it.

00:52:45.090 --> 00:52:47.850
I use it-- I find myself using it more and
more.

00:52:47.850 --> 00:52:54.580
But because I was so used to Base R, it's
been hard for me to switch over.

00:52:54.580 --> 00:52:59.090
And ggplot, as implemented in R, basically
has two steps.

00:52:59.090 --> 00:53:04.530
One is you have to work really hard to clean
up your data and get it into a nice format.

00:53:04.530 --> 00:53:09.940
And that's almost a feature of ggplot in that
it forces you to work really hard on your

00:53:09.940 --> 00:53:12.570
pre-processing before you start plotting.

00:53:12.570 --> 00:53:17.170
And then once you get it in a nice format,
then you're off to the races with the plots.

00:53:17.170 --> 00:53:22.740
And then the actual plotting syntax is quite
good for ggplot.

00:53:22.740 --> 00:53:24.100
So I would recommend that.

00:53:24.100 --> 00:53:34.620
But I would say, every statistical data analysis
program has completely tolerable graphics

00:53:34.620 --> 00:53:35.620
capabilities.

00:53:35.620 --> 00:53:41.200
And it's more, I think-- I don't think the
tool is as much the problem.

00:53:41.200 --> 00:53:46.190
Now, if you're talking about creating production-ready
graphics, then I think you can get into the

00:53:46.190 --> 00:53:50.390
specifics of various platforms.

00:53:50.390 --> 00:53:56.570
One thing that I didn't mention that is quite
useful is interactive graphics.

00:53:56.570 --> 00:54:00.720
That's where you create a plot, and you create
sliders or buttons or dialog boxes and things

00:54:00.720 --> 00:54:05.230
like that so that you-- so that the person
who's looking at the plot, whether it's you,

00:54:05.230 --> 00:54:09.340
or whether you're giving it to someone else,
can interact with it.

00:54:09.340 --> 00:54:14.220
And there is increasingly great tools for
that.

00:54:14.220 --> 00:54:15.460
Plotly is an example.

00:54:15.460 --> 00:54:17.980
Shiny in R is an example for that.

00:54:17.980 --> 00:54:22.960
And then, of course, the gold standard for
that is D3, which is a JavaScript library.

00:54:22.960 --> 00:54:30.060
But that requires a pretty heavy investment
of programming knowledge and time to master

00:54:30.060 --> 00:54:31.060
that.

00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:34.770
But there's some layers, like Plotly, that
have been built on top of D3 and other libraries

00:54:34.770 --> 00:54:36.670
that make it kind of easy.

00:54:36.670 --> 00:54:42.050
So at any rate, I guess the answer to the
question, from my perspective, I use R, and

00:54:42.050 --> 00:54:48.350
I like R. And then if I needed interactive
graphics, I would use Shiny or Plotly.

00:54:48.350 --> 00:54:50.560
Thanks.

00:54:50.560 --> 00:54:56.940
And there was a comment in here, not along
the lines of software, but other resources.

00:54:56.940 --> 00:55:06.920
There was a comment here from Ethan McMann
about a report-- a CIA report that he's seen

00:55:06.920 --> 00:55:09.800
that is useful in this area.

00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:17.080
Can you, in addition to your comments on software,
could you give some advice about where someone

00:55:17.080 --> 00:55:20.230
might go to find out more information about
EDA?

00:55:20.230 --> 00:55:26.580
I mean, you mentioned some of the leaders
in this area, like Hadley Wickham, and Karl

00:55:26.580 --> 00:55:28.300
Broman, Jenny Bryan, Bill Cleveland.

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:31.800
And you also mentioned Roger Peng's Coursera
course.

00:55:31.800 --> 00:55:38.530
But other than those, going to the research
of the first group, or Roger's course, do

00:55:38.530 --> 00:55:42.900
you have any other advice for where to find
more resources?

00:55:42.900 --> 00:55:46.080
So-
Than the tolerable book that you could just

00:55:46.080 --> 00:55:47.080
pick up.

00:55:47.080 --> 00:55:50.750
Meaning anything that really stands out.

00:55:50.750 --> 00:55:57.020
So there's two aspects to EDA, and one is
super well-developed, and the other is a little

00:55:57.020 --> 00:55:58.660
more amorphous.

00:55:58.660 --> 00:56:05.260
So the first aspect to exploratory data analysis
is the plotting component to it.

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:09.750
And there is, I think, a lot of great materials
on plotting.

00:56:09.750 --> 00:56:12.880
So on design, there is Tufte's books.

00:56:12.880 --> 00:56:16.240
On the perception, there's all this Cleveland
work.

00:56:16.240 --> 00:56:20.630
On the implementation, there's lots of books
on specific plotting implementation.

00:56:20.630 --> 00:56:29.440
There was a great book written by Nathan Yau,
who does FlowingData.

00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:32.830
Look at his work on graphs.

00:56:32.830 --> 00:56:36.640
I'm trying to think.

00:56:36.640 --> 00:56:42.690
Roger Peng's course has quite a bit of stuff
on graphing.

00:56:42.690 --> 00:56:45.190
Cleveland actually has a book on plotting
as well.

00:56:45.190 --> 00:56:49.910
Kind of a Tufte style book on plotting.

00:56:49.910 --> 00:56:52.070
There is a-- boy, I'm forgetting.

00:56:52.070 --> 00:56:53.570
I'm blanking on some names.

00:56:53.570 --> 00:56:57.440
But there's lots of books on plotting.

00:56:57.440 --> 00:57:01.140
And then there's the other aspect of exploratory
data analysis, which I didn't get too far

00:57:01.140 --> 00:57:11.690
into, which is you could use models, clustering,
huge chunk of tools, what I would just call

00:57:11.690 --> 00:57:13.100
modeling tools.

00:57:13.100 --> 00:57:19.410
But in general, statistical tools can also
be part of exploratory data analysis.

00:57:19.410 --> 00:57:22.330
Regression is a key part of exploratory data
analysis.

00:57:22.330 --> 00:57:23.330
Right?

00:57:23.330 --> 00:57:28.950
You fit, add a model, throw in a confounder,
check things.

00:57:28.950 --> 00:57:33.820
And I would say that's less well-developed
as a field.

00:57:33.820 --> 00:57:40.260
So there, you have to go to books on machine
learning or cluster to look at unsupervised

00:57:40.260 --> 00:57:41.270
clustering.

00:57:41.270 --> 00:57:48.710
Any regression class will have lectures on
how to use regression, but they won't differentiate

00:57:48.710 --> 00:57:53.860
the use of regression in EDA versus the use
of regression in a final confirmatory-type

00:57:53.860 --> 00:57:57.140
analysis.

00:57:57.140 --> 00:57:59.790
The standard for that is, of course, Tukey's
book.

00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:03.230
For that style of analysis, it's Tukey's book.

00:58:03.230 --> 00:58:04.610
It's wonderful in that regard.

00:58:04.610 --> 00:58:08.270
So that would be the starting point, and then
I'll think about that.

00:58:08.270 --> 00:58:14.360
Maybe on the slides, which are Google
Docs, I'll add some references if I can think

00:58:14.360 --> 00:58:15.360
of some more.

00:58:15.360 --> 00:58:16.360
OK.

00:58:16.360 --> 00:58:21.160
And I think you just answered the last question,
which was commenting on whether some other

00:58:21.160 --> 00:58:26.080
techniques, like dimension reduction and collective
variable search would be considered EDA or

00:58:26.080 --> 00:58:28.490
could be categorized as EDA.

00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:31.900
And that sounds like what you just answered.

00:58:31.900 --> 00:58:32.900
Yes.

00:58:32.900 --> 00:58:33.900
Absolutely.

00:58:33.900 --> 00:58:35.680
Absolutely, I would consider that EDA.

00:58:35.680 --> 00:58:42.950
Anything-- EDA, I guess one way to define
EDA is anything that's not confirmatory in

00:58:42.950 --> 00:58:44.820
data analysis.

00:58:44.820 --> 00:58:51.290
But I think when I look at my definition,
it's a little bit-- it's not very strict for

00:58:51.290 --> 00:58:53.330
what constitutes EDA.

00:58:53.330 --> 00:58:58.820
And I think I define it more along the lines
of if you're really doing hypothesis generation,

00:58:58.820 --> 00:59:04.240
and you're really doing a more free-flowing
style of analysis, where you're looking for

00:59:04.240 --> 00:59:09.700
trends, and you're poking and you're prodding,
and you're developing your hypotheses on the

00:59:09.700 --> 00:59:10.700
fly.

00:59:10.700 --> 00:59:14.930
Anytime you do that, no matter what techniques
you're using, I think you're doing EDA.

00:59:14.930 --> 00:59:15.940
OK.

00:59:15.940 --> 00:59:16.950
Great.

00:59:16.950 --> 00:59:23.280
And with that, since we are out of time, I
want to thank you again for this great lecture

00:59:23.280 --> 00:59:27.660
and for coming here to help us understand
this better.

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:28.900
Thank you, Brian.

00:59:28.900 --> 00:59:29.900
No problem.

00:59:29.900 --> 00:59:30.900
Thank you.

00:59:30.900 --> 00:59:31.900
All right.

00:59:31.900 --> 00:59:32.900
Bye-bye.

00:59:32.900 --> 00:59:32.901
Bye.

